#open-question(title: [What "Recovery" Means: Three Incommensurate Referents])[
The recovery debate in ME/CFS partly dissolves when its central term is disambiguated.
"Recovery" is used to mean at least three incommensurate outcomes: full remission to
premorbid functioning — the bar physicians apply @Devendorf2019RecoveryPhysician; full
recovery, reached by roughly one in four patients after CBT, versus exit from diagnostic
criteria, reached by over two in three in the same cohort @Knoop2007FullRecoveryCBT;
and self-rated "better", reported by about 44% after CBT @Ingman2022RecoveryRCTReview. The
"recovery conceivable" framing adds a fourth referent that is neither a biological endpoint
nor a functional threshold: a shift in illness understanding coupled with felt improvement and
renewed hope @Bakken2026RecoveryConceivable.

The consequence is that much of the efficacy dispute — does a psychobehavioural approach
"recover" patients — is contested at the level of the word itself, not the underlying data:
the same study can be read as showing that recovery is a minority outcome (full remission),
a common outcome (self-rated improvement), or a matter of orientation rather than outcome
(conceivability). Whether any of these is the "right" referent, and whether a single measure
can serve patients, clinicians, and researchers at once, remains an open conceptual question.

*Consequence:* A patient and a clinician can both be honest while disagreeing entirely about
whether "recovery" happened — because they are measuring different things. Naming the three
referents is a prerequisite for any claim about recovery rates to mean anything.

] <oq:recovery-definitional-drift>
